pr_body:
Fixes #53080

PEReader had a cache for section blocks but never used it.  It would always read the new block before checking the cache.

I wrote a rudimentary benchmark for this:
```C#
using PEReader pe = new PEReader(File.OpenRead(typeof(object).Assembly.Location));

var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++)
{
    var section = pe.GetSectionData(".text");
}
sw.Stop();
Console.WriteLine(sw.Elapsed);
```


Before change:
00:00:00.0102182

After change:
00:00:00.0090209

10x reduction seems worth it.

comments:
It reproduces with this short program (full project: [SRMPerfTest.zip](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/files/6804399/SRMPerfTest.zip)):
```c#
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Reflection.Metadata;
using System.Reflection.PortableExecutable;

using var fs = new FileStream(typeof(PEReader).Assembly.Location, FileMode.Open);
using var pe = new PEReader(fs, PEStreamOptions.Default);
var reader = pe.GetMetadataReader();
int totalMethodSizes = 0;
int numMethodBodies = 0;
foreach (var mh in reader.MethodDefinitions)
{
    var md = reader.GetMethodDefinition(mh);
    if (md.RelativeVirtualAddress != 0)
    {
        var body = pe.GetMethodBody(md.RelativeVirtualAddress);
        totalMethodSizes += body.Size;
        numMethodBodies++;
    }
}
Console.WriteLine($"numMethodBodies = {numMethodBodies}");
Console.WriteLine($"totalMethodSizes = {totalMethodSizes}");
```

Publish for linux, and run:
```
$ ./SRMPerfTest
numMethodBodies = 3355
totalMethodSizes = 148711
$ strace ./SRMPerfTest |& grep msync | wc -l
3356
```

There's one msync call when loading the metadata from the assembly, and then another call for each method body.
For me the program takes 3.35s counting the method bodies in System.Reflection.Metadata.dll.

The bug can be worked around by using `PEStreamOptions.PrefetchEntireImage`. This avoids the `msync` calls completely and reduces the run-time to 2.24s. I think the `msync` doesn't actually flush the disk cache on Linux (unlike what was reported on MacOS, which I can't test on) so the performance impact isn't as catastrophic.

I believe the fix would be something like this change to `PEReader.GetPESectionBlock`:
```diff
            if (_lazyPESectionBlocks == null)
            {
                Interlocked.CompareExchange(ref _lazyPESectionBlocks, new AbstractMemoryBlock[PEHeaders.SectionHeaders.Length], null);
            }

+         var exitingBlock = Volatile.Read(ref _lazyPESectionBlocks[index]);
+         if (existingBlock != null) return existingBlock;

            AbstractMemoryBlock newBlock;
```
but I haven't tested that yet.
